Z||000005||88 Fiero||10-27-1999||10:29 AM||||You can buy that module for windows in any alarm installation shop...Actually I have a buddy that he install this module in his fiero and it also works on the passenger side window. The good part about it is that it also hooks up with the alarm system.. During summer and you want to ventilate the inside of your Fiero...just push on of the buttons and both windows will move down about 1/2 and inch to ventilate....||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-27-1999||03:22 PM||||seems to me the link that started this is allot of work for what it does.<P>Many of the systems I've seen use a switch with "second touch". You push lightly on the switch and the window works as normal, push it all the way down for auto open.<P>If that type of switch has a version that would fit in Fiero then adding the auto open becomes very simple. You would use the second touch to activate a latch, and then use a current probe to deactivate it. The latch operates a relay that is wired across the switch. Your looking at a circuit that takes one chip, a transistor, relay, and a hall effect switch. Maybe a few resistor and capacitors. No programming at all. You don't even need much power regulation if you use CMOS.<P>even without the second touch switch you could use about the same simple circuit. You would need to make it so that hitting the oposite direction cancels it tho. <P>You'd also want to put in a very slight activation delay so the window doesn't drop if you bump the button. that's easy to do with just an added resistor and capacitor.<P>If you do it in both directions, then the activation delay is important. otherwise when you tap the switch to cancel, the thing would reverse.<P>Auto close is kind of dangerous if not very carefully done. you could end up cutting off some kids air by the time you load the motor enough to stop it.||||reg||1
